  "final_text": "I finished all four steps and checked each one by reading it back from Kibana.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has the time field `timestamp`. Its other fields are:\n   - **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n   - **Keywords:** `Carrier`, `FlightNum`, `FlightTimeHour`, `FlightDelayType`, and the airport ID, city, country, region and weather fields for both origin and destination (`Origin*`, `Dest*`)\n   - **Booleans:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n   - **Locations:** `OriginLocation`, `DestLocation`\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-5c3c5794 Flights' (id `19d3ca4c-dc0e-42bd-9548-6b5233eb8f0d`) has exactly two bar charts on the data view, both counting flights:\n   - \"Flights over time\", by `timestamp`\n   - a chart by `Carrier`, showing the top 10 carriers\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-5c3c5794 Cancelled flights' (id `1e93f1bc-ff57-40da-af6a-49526f885800`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It looks for `Cancelled: true`, runs every minute over a 5-minute window, and fires when the count is more than 10. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\", and the other panel didn't change. Reading the rule back shows it enabled, running every minute, with its last run status \"ok\".\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
